## The console's Caddy block (zylos/README.md "The Caddy block"). Lines
## starting with ## are notes and are left out by `node zylos/lib.js caddy`,
## which prints the block with this host's values in place of <NAME>,
## <BASE_PATH>, <PORT>, <AUTH_SOCK>, <USERS_FILE> and <USER_HEADER>. Paste the
## printed block inside the primary site block of ~/zylos/http/Caddyfile, just
## before its closing }, keeping the BEGIN/END lines (zylos uninstall
## removes the block by them).
##
## - handle_path strips <BASE_PATH>: the console's links are all relative.
## - The public Host is passed through unchanged (no `header_up Host`): the
##   console compares Origin with Host, and answers only a Host named by
##   --allow-host (console.allow_host_key).
## - The login is a form (zylos/bin/login.js on the unix socket <AUTH_SOCK>),
##   not basic_auth: a browser or password manager can save a form.
##   /login and /logout go to it; everything else first asks it
##   (forward_auth, GET /_auth) whether the visitor has a session: yes, the
##   console is reached with <USER_HEADER> set to the user (and no Cookie: the
##   session is the login's business, not the console's); no, the visitor gets
##   the form. <USERS_FILE> holds the logins (`node zylos/lib.js passwd NAME`).
## - `route` keeps the written order: `request_header -<USER_HEADER>` drops
##   any copy the visitor sent before forward_auth sets the real one.
# BEGIN zylos-component:<NAME>
redir <BASE_PATH> <BASE_PATH>/ permanent
handle_path <BASE_PATH>/* {
    route {
        request_header -<USER_HEADER>
        @login path /login /logout
        handle @login {
            reverse_proxy unix/<AUTH_SOCK>
        }
        handle {
            forward_auth unix/<AUTH_SOCK> {
                uri /_auth
                copy_headers <USER_HEADER>
            }
            reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:<PORT> {
                header_up -Cookie
            }
        }
    }
}
# END zylos-component:<NAME>
